<section class="section" id="how" aria-labelledby="how-title">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">How it works</p>
    <h2 id="how-title">One cable out to the yard, all the thinking indoors</h2>
    <p>
      The box outdoors runs no AI. It captures and streams, which keeps it simple and cool. The computer
      indoors does the recognising and keeps the records.
    </p>
  </div>
  <ol class="falcon-flow">
    <li class="falcon-flow__step">
      <h3><span>Outdoors</span>The node</h3>
      <p>A Raspberry Pi takes video from the camera, sound from the microphones and readings from the weather and rain sensors.</p>
    </li>
    <li class="falcon-flow__link">One Ethernet cable. Power goes out; video, sound and readings come back.</li>
    <li class="falcon-flow__step">
      <h3><span>Indoors</span>The computer</h3>
      <p>A PC with a graphics card finds animals in the picture, suggests a species, names bird calls and stores each visit with its clip and the weather.</p>
    </li>
    <li class="falcon-flow__link">Home Wi-Fi</li>
    <li class="falcon-flow__step">
      <h3><span>Phone or PC</span>The dashboard</h3>
      <p>A web page for watching live, reviewing doubtful records and exporting the checked ones.</p>
    </li>
  </ol>
  <div class="falcon-two">
    <div>
      <h3>What runs where</h3>
      <ul class="falcon-list">
        <li><b>On the node:</b> a small Python service and MediaMTX, which serve the video and sound to the one computer the node is paired with. A heat guard pauses the stream if the Pi gets too hot.</li>
        <li><b>Finding animals:</b> MegaDetector marks animals, people and vehicles in the picture.</li>
        <li><b>Naming species:</b> BioCLIP 2.5 compares each cropped animal with the species on the Rajasthan list.</li>
        <li><b>Naming calls:</b> BirdNET, limited to about 257 birds expected around Kota in the season.</li>
        <li><b>The application:</b> a Python backend with a SQLite database and a React dashboard, with about 700 automated tests across the backend, the node service and the dashboard.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>What stays at home</h3>
      <p>
        The models run on the computer indoors. No picture, sound or record is sent to a cloud service for
        recognition. Checked records leave only when the owner exports them, as a
        <a href="https://camtrap-dp.tdwg.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Camtrap DP</a> package,
        the open format used for camera-trap data.
      </p>
      <p class="falcon-note">
        The export passes the standard's own checks. It has not yet been loaded into another person's tools,
        so it is not claimed to be research-ready.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

