<section class="section" id="hardware" aria-labelledby="hardware-title">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Hardware</p>
    <h2 id="hardware-title">Off-the-shelf parts in a hand-drilled box</h2>
    <p>
      Every part of Build 1 is something you can buy. The box was drilled by hand and the camera mount is
      made by hand. That shell is the part to replace first.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Part</th><th scope="col">What it is</th><th scope="col">Its job</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {% for row in f.hardware %}
        <tr><th scope="row">{{ row.part }}</th><td>{{ row.what }}</td><td>{{ row.job }}</td></tr>
        {% endfor %}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="rules" aria-labelledby="rules-title">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">How it decides</p>
    <h2 id="rules-title">A name has to be earned</h2>
    <p>
      The first nights produced a flood of false sightings. The rule that came out of them is simple:
      a few records you can trust are worth more than many doubtful ones.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="falcon-tiers">
    <article class="falcon-tier falcon-tier--likely">
      <h3>Likely</h3>
      <p>Several frames over about two seconds agree on one species, clearly ahead of the next best name. Only these appear on the Live page.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="falcon-tier falcon-tier--probable">
      <h3>Probable or possible</h3>
      <p>Look-alikes score almost the same, or the guess is faint. These wait on the Review page and are never announced.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="falcon-tier falcon-tier--confirmed">
      <h3>Confirmed</h3>
      <p>Only a person can confirm a record, after watching its clip or listening to its sound.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
  <ul class="falcon-list">
    <li>The number shown beside a name is a visual similarity score. It is not a probability, and the dashboard says so.</li>
    <li>Things that stay put, such as the bowl or a stone, are learned as scenery and ignored.</li>
    <li>A strip at the edge of the picture is ignored, and one frame alone is never enough.</li>
    <li>A person's review does not retrain the models. Discarding a record hides it and can be undone.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="section" id="results" aria-labelledby="results-title">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Results so far</p>
    <h2 id="results-title">The first week at the bowl</h2>
    <p>
      Build 1 was put together between 27 October and 10 November 2026, and the node has watched the bowl
      since the evening of 7 November. These are notes from one yard over about a week. They are not a
      study. Each note says who or what the name comes from.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="falcon-results">
    <article class="falcon-result">
      <span class="falcon-evidence falcon-evidence--person">Checked by a person</span>
      <h3>A small cat on the first night</h3>
      <p>
        On 7 November at 19:04 a small cat came to the bowl, twice. The species model named it Rusty-spotted
        Cat over 94 frames, and I confirmed that from the clip. Infrared video of a small cat is hard to
        judge, so a second opinion from someone who knows small cats is welcome.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="falcon-result">
      <span class="falcon-evidence falcon-evidence--model">Model suggestions, mostly unreviewed</span>
      <h3>Birds by day</h3>
      <p>
        By day the camera's suggestions at the bowl include Indian Robin and Oriental Magpie-Robin, and on
        14 November a Bluethroat came to bathe. Most daytime records are still waiting for review, so this
        is a list of suggestions, not a checked list.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="falcon-result">
      <span class="falcon-evidence falcon-evidence--unsure">By sound, marked unsure</span>
      <h3>Owls and a heron after dark</h3>
      <p>
        At night the microphones logged owl calls and a Gray Heron that the camera never saw. The names
        are BirdNET's. I cannot tell the owls apart by ear, so those records stay marked unsure.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="falcon-result">
      <span class="falcon-evidence falcon-evidence--model">Machine scores, not verified</span>
      <h3>One morning hour, as the machine heard it</h3>
      <p>
        Between 07:22 and 08:22 on 8 November BirdNET's highest scores were Red-wattled Lapwing (0.84),
        Eurasian Coot (0.77), Rose-ringed Parakeet (0.76), Gray Heron (0.61) and Black-winged Stilt
        (0.60). No person has checked these.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>

  <h3 class="falcon-subhead">What went wrong, and what changed</h3>
  <ul class="falcon-lessons">
    <li>
      <p><b>What happened</b>On the first night, 133 visits were house geckos, filed under four wrong gecko names.</p>
      <p><b>What changed</b>A name now needs several agreeing frames. When the model cannot settle on one group of animals, the record stays hidden as unidentified.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p><b>What happened</b>A “palm civet” turned out to be a fixed shape at the edge of the picture. The edge of the camera hood, glowing in infrared, was read as a snake.</p>
      <p><b>What changed</b>A strip at the edge of the picture is ignored, things that stay put are treated as scenery, and one frame alone is not enough.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p><b>What happened</b>The sound model offered Tawny Owl, a bird that does not live in Rajasthan.</p>
      <p><b>What changed</b>The site was set to Kota, and BirdNET now chooses only from birds expected there in the season.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p><b>What happened</b>On some mornings the camera stays in its night setting after sunrise and the picture turns pink. Its light sensor sits in the shade of the hood.</p>
      <p><b>Still open</b>Software cannot fix this. The sensor has to see daylight, which means a different hood.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p><b>What happened</b>On 14 November the clip of a bathing Bluethroat stopped after 10 seconds, while the bird stayed longer.</p>
      <p><b>In progress</b>Recordings are being changed to last as long as the animal stays.</p>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <div class="falcon-two">
    <div>
      <h3 class="falcon-subhead">Measured on this rig</h3>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">What</th><th scope="col">Measured</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Video from the node</th><td>1920 × 1080 at up to 15 frames a second, about 14 Mbit/s</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Raspberry Pi temperature</th><td>59 to 61 °C when it passes the camera's video through untouched; up to 77 °C when it compressed the video itself</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Naming one cropped animal</th><td>about 45 ms on the PC's graphics card</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Air at the camera over one day</th><td>about 18 °C before dawn to about 30 °C in the afternoon (13 to 14 November)</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Waiting for review on 14 November</th><td>959 records</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3 class="falcon-subhead">Known limits</h3>
      <ul class="falcon-list">
        <li>Night vision is weak. The infrared light reaches about a metre, and insects in it cannot be named.</li>
        <li>The models guess. The rules cut down false records, but they cannot make a model certain.</li>
        <li>A person still has to review, and the backlog shows that this is the slowest part.</li>
        <li>It needs a PC with an NVIDIA graphics card indoors.</li>
        <li>The box is drilled by hand and is no longer sealed. It has not been through a monsoon or a summer.</li>
        <li>Sound from a residential street includes traffic and voices.</li>
        <li>It logs visits to one spot. It does not count animals or estimate populations.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="roadmap" aria-labelledby="roadmap-title">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Where it is going</p>
    <h2 id="roadmap-title">From a backyard rig to something others can host</h2>
    <p>
      The aim is a device that a friend, a school or a field researcher can plug in and use without a
      desktop PC or any technical setup. Hardware is bought one piece at a time, and each step has to pass
      a test before the next one starts.
    </p>
  </div>
  <ol class="falcon-roadmap">
    {% for step in f.roadmap %}
    {% set stateClass = "falcon-state--" + (step.state | lower | replace(" ", "-")) %}
    <li class="{{ stateClass }}">
      <span class="falcon-state {{ stateClass }}">{{ step.state }}</span>
      <h3>{{ step.name }}</h3>
      <p>{{ step.text }}</p>
    </li>
    {% endfor %}
  </ol>
</section>

<section class="section falcon-support" id="support" aria-labelledby="support-title">
  <div class="falcon-support__panel">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Support the next build</p>
      <h2 id="support-title">Two pieces of equipment would move this forward</h2>
      <p>
        Build 1 leans on a desktop PC and a shell made by hand. Two purchases would turn
        it into something other people can copy, and I am asking readers to help with them. Prices are what
        Indian sellers listed on {{ f.pricesChecked }}.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="falcon-asks">
      <article class="falcon-ask">
        <h3>A small computer for the AI</h3>
        <p class="falcon-ask__price">about ₹65,000 to ₹70,000</p>
        <dl>
          <div>
            <dt>What</dt>
            <dd>An NVIDIA Jetson Orin Nano Super developer kit with a solid-state drive, a fan case and a Wi-Fi card. The kit alone was listed between ₹52,000 and ₹69,300 at three sellers.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Why</dt>
            <dd>Today every model runs on a desktop PC with a gaming graphics card. A home, a school or a field station without one cannot host a node.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>What is not known yet</dt>
            <dd>FALCON has never run on a Jetson. The models have to be converted for it, and it must give the same answers as the PC on nights already recorded before it replaces the PC. It may not manage that. If it does not, this page will say so.</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </article>
      <article class="falcon-ask">
        <h3>A simple 3D printer</h3>
        <p class="falcon-ask__price">about ₹25,000 to ₹30,000</p>
        <dl>
          <div>
            <dt>What</dt>
            <dd>A small desktop printer such as the Bambu Lab A1 mini, with filament for outdoor parts. The printer alone was listed between ₹17,300 and ₹21,500.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Why</dt>
            <dd>A hood that lets the light sensor see daylight, a shaded holder for the weather sensor, a cradle for the rain plate and a post mount. Printed parts can be made the same way twice and shared as files.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>What is not known yet</dt>
            <dd>The repository holds a printable enclosure designed for an earlier camera. It has never been printed. Which plastic survives a Kota summer is part of the test.</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="falcon-total">
      <p><strong>{{ f.fund.goal }}</strong>for both, with the smaller parts they need.</p>
      <a class="button-link" href="{{ f.supportUrl }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Contribute on Buy Me a Coffee</a>
    </div>

    <dl class="falcon-tally" aria-label="Running total">
      <div><dt>Received for FALCON</dt><dd>{{ f.fund.received }}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Spent</dt><dd>{{ f.fund.spent }}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Bought so far</dt><dd>Nothing yet</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Updated</dt><dd>{{ f.fund.updated }}</dd></div>
    </dl>

    <div class="falcon-terms">
      <div>
        <h3>How the money is handled</h3>
        <ul class="falcon-list">
          <li>Contributions go through the Cane &amp; Camera page on Buy Me a Coffee, the same page that supports the upkeep of MHTR.in. <b>Please write FALCON in your message</b> so the money is counted here. Without that word it goes to the upkeep of the site, as before.</li>
          <li>Money marked FALCON is spent only on the two items above. The printer comes first, because it costs less and is useful at once.</li>
          <li>The running total above is updated by hand whenever money comes in or something is bought.</li>
          <li>If less comes in than the computer costs, that money stays set aside for it, and this page will say how much is there.</li>
          <li>This is a personal project, not a registered charity. A contribution is a gift: there are no rewards, no shares, no device in return and no tax benefit.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Other ways to help</h3>
        <ul class="falcon-list">
          <li><b>Lend hardware.</b> A Jetson you no longer use, or a few hours on your own 3D printer, does the same job as money.</li>
          <li><b>Lend your ears.</b> If you know the night calls of owls around Kota and can check a few short recordings, write to me.</li>
          <li><b>Try the code.</b> Follow the install guide on your own computer and report where it breaks.</li>
          <li><b>Look at the cat.</b> If you know small cats, I would like a second opinion on the clip from 7 November.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="falcon-note">Write to <a href="mailto:{{ site.contactEmail }}">{{ site.contactEmail }}</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="code" aria-labelledby="code-title">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Open source</p>
    <h2 id="code-title">Build one, or help build the next</h2>
    <p>
      Everything written for FALCON is in one public repository: the node service for the Raspberry Pi, the
      application that runs the models, the dashboard, the tests and the build notes. Build 1 is tagged v1.0.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="falcon-two">
    <div>
      <ul class="falcon-list">
        <li>The FALCON code is under the Apache 2.0 licence. You can use it, change it and share it.</li>
        <li>The models are not part of the repository. FALCON downloads them, and each keeps its own licence.</li>
        <li>The BirdNET model is for non-commercial use only. Anyone thinking of selling a device has to replace it or license it.</li>
        <li>Changes come in as pull requests.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="button-link" href="{{ f.repoUrl }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FALCON on GitHub</a>
        <a class="button-link button-link-secondary" href="{{ f.repoUrl }}/tree/v1.0" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Build 1 (v1.0)</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <dl class="falcon-licences" aria-label="Licences">
      <div><dt>FALCON code</dt><dd>Apache 2.0</dd></div>
      <div><dt>MegaDetector V6</dt><dd>MIT variant, through PyTorchWildlife. Finds animals, people and vehicles.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>BioCLIP 2.5</dt><dd>MIT. Suggests the species.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>BirdNET V2.4</dt><dd>Code MIT; model CC BY-NC-SA 4.0, which means non-commercial use only.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>MediaMTX</dt><dd>MIT. Serves the video and sound from the node.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Species list</dt><dd>Built from research-grade records on iNaturalist.</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="care" aria-labelledby="care-title">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Care and privacy</p>
    <h2 id="care-title">It watches wildlife, not people</h2>
  </div>
  <ul class="falcon-list">
    <li>People are never saved as observations. By default a person in the picture is covered with a grey box, live and in saved clips, and seconds with human voices are silenced in saved sound. These filters are automatic and can miss, so anyone who hosts a node should still tell the people who live there and the neighbours within earshot.</li>
    <li>The models run on the owner's computer. No picture or sound is sent to a cloud service for recognition.</li>
    <li>This node stands in a private backyard in Kota city. Using a device like it inside a protected area needs written permission from the Forest Department.</li>
    <li>This page gives the place only as Kota, Rajasthan. Exact locations of wildlife records are not published here.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

